sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: docs/cli/firewall/index.mdx; docs/reference/cli-ux.md; ADR 0244 and ADR 0241; Swamp shape; command development pipeline stage 1.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: firewall:deny
  %% kind: firewall.deny
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: firewall:deny PORT<br/>--node=NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit firewall:deny PORT --node=NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - one destination port from 1 through 65535, or an ordered inclusive range; service names are refused (`firewall.port_invalid`)
  %%> - a positive numeric Node ID (`firewall.node_id_invalid`)
  %%> - without optional flags: deny both TCP and UDP from any source, in both address families
  %%> - the CLI generates one idempotency key per logical start and reuses it for transport retries; an intentional new attempt gets a new key
  %%> Prompts:
  %%> - in a terminal, ask for an omitted port with the text prompt `Port`; ask again after an invalid answer
  %%> - select an omitted Node with the data list `Node`: active Linux Nodes, columns `ID` and `Name`, numeric IDs as selectors, and `Press / to search`; never infer a target
  %%> - show waiting feedback for a slow lookup; no eligible Nodes returns `firewall.node_id_invalid`
  %%> - Ctrl-C or end of input exits 1 with `input.cancelled`, before admission
  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, missing input exits 1 with the corresponding input error and creates no Operation
  opt --idempotency-key=KEY
    %% path: idempotency-key
    Note over GW: Replay a keyed start
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - an explicit key overrides the internally generated key
    %%> - after access checks, the same key, target and input return the recorded Operation without another run, even if it failed
    %%> - different input or target returns `operation.idempotency_conflict`; a malformed key returns `validation.failed`
    %%> - replay follows the existing Operation below; it does not perform the admission and Node work again
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - an intentional repair is a new start, not a replay of a failed Operation
  end
  alt new start
    %% path: new-start
    Note over GW: Validate, record the rule
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node (`node_access.required`); the target is an active Linux Node (`firewall.platform_unsupported` for another platform)
    %%> - the port or range includes no protected port, regardless of source or protocol: SSH 22, recovery SSH, WireGuard, or a port needed by a role on the target (`firewall.protected_port`)
    %%> - no opposite action for the same port, protocol and normalized source (`firewall.rule_conflict`)
    %%> - no both-protocol rule overlapping a single-protocol rule on the same port and source, in either direction (`firewall.rule_overlap`)
    %%> - no other firewall change runs on the target (`operation.target_busy`); one lock per Node
    %%> Allowed:
    %%> - different source scopes may overlap: a narrow allow overrides a broad deny, and a narrow deny overrides a broad allow
    %%> - repeat an active or failed deny: reuse its identity record and check the Node in a new Operation
    %%> Result:
    %%> - a refusal creates no Operation or rule and changes nothing on the Node
    %%> - an admitted new start records the rule as provisioning and captures the full non-secret intent; no Node work has happened yet
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-firewall-direct
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the exact rule tuple and comment, target address and pinned SSH host keys
    %%> - the shared SSH identity comes from the worker's swamp role; no step calls the Gateway
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the CLI follows the Operation with `operation:show`
    SW->>N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status · @swamp/ssh exec
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads numbered UFW rows over official SSH; needed to plan insert positions and detect drift on a repeat
    %%> - inactive UFW fails with `firewall.backend_inactive` before mutation; never enables the firewall
    Note over SW: Plan the changes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - plans exact missing lines and moves misplaced lines; an identical, well-placed line is left untouched
    %%> - narrower sources go first: one address, then CIDRs by prefix length, then anywhere; operator lines go above Orbit's broad managed rules
    %%> - plans one deny per protocol and applicable address family; a port range stays a range
    %%> - numbered positions account for earlier planned changes
    %%> Why native:
    %%> - official cfgmgmt firewall cannot insert at a position or express a range; each mutation is one UFW argv over official SSH, with no copied script
    alt changes needed
      %% path: changes-needed
      SW->>N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv · @swamp/ssh exec
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - executes planned changes sequentially; a native guard skips entries requiring no work
      %%> - moves a misplaced line by deleting and reinserting it; preserves unrelated rules
    else already applied
      %% path: already-applied
      Note over SW: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists
      %%> Result:
      %%> - no mutation; the final read and check must still pass before reporting a successful skip
    end
    SW->>N: Read the result<br/>ufw status · @swamp/ssh exec
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - collects the final rows for the one end-state check
    Note over SW: Check the rules
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - a typed check compares every expected line and its narrow-first position; native assert requires the check to pass
    %%> - the public result comes from the check, not a separate report step
    %%> Failure:
    %%> - a missing or misplaced line fails with `firewall.verify_failed`, including a change made between mutation and this read
    alt rules present and ordered
      %% path: succeeded
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the Gateway's shared reader marks the rule active and the Operation succeeded
      %%> - only a no-change plan reports `Skipped — already applied (firewall.rule_exists)` in human and JSON results
    else read, apply or check fails
      %% path: failed
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code · failed step
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the rule stays visible as failed; the Operation fails and the CLI exits 1
      %%> - SSH, UFW mutation or another native step stopping before a public check result is `operation.native_failed`
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - previously added lines remain, and a line deleted for reordering may be absent; there is no rollback
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - fix the cause and repeat the same command as a new start; reuse the record, keep correct lines and repair missing or misplaced ones without duplicates
    end
  else keyed replay
    %% path: keyed-replay
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>recorded Operation
    %%> Result:
    %%> - access and the full key fingerprint matched; return the same Operation, without rule changes or another run
    %%> - the CLI follows that Operation, including a previously recorded failure; this is not a repair or an already-applied skip
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads recorded progress and the final result; does not start more Node work
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - human output follows progress and shows the final outcome, Operation and request IDs; piped output has no animation or escape codes
  end
  opt --json
    %% path: json
    Note over CLI: Print the final JSON
    %%> Result:
    %%> - one final Operation, or an error envelope for a refusal; no prompts, human progress or ANSI codes
    %%> - successful skips keep their stable reason code; execution failures remain failed Operations, not admission errors
  end
